{"title":"KVPY","category":"Engineering","bank":"kvpy","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"d and f Block Elements","count":9,"questions":[{"id":51103,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"d and f Block Elements","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"The gas produced from thermal decomposition of $\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{4}\\right)_{2} \\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{7}$ is","options":["oxygen","nitric oxide","ammonia","nitrogen"],"answer":3,"solution":"$\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{4}\\right)_{2} \\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{7} \\stackrel{\\Delta}{\\longrightarrow} \\mathrm{N}_{2}+\\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{3}+4 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2011 (SA)","_marksId":"6174d21351e5c0c495e1106a"},{"id":51104,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"d and f Block Elements","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"The most abundant transition metal in human body is","options":["copper","iron","zinc","manganese"],"answer":1,"solution":"Most abundant element (transition) in human body is Iron.","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2015 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d213599762c8854c0d7d"},{"id":51105,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"d and f Block Elements","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"Among the following metals, the strongest reducing agent is","options":["$\\mathrm{Ni}$","$\\mathrm{Cu}$","$\\mathrm{Zn}$","$\\mathrm{Fe}$"],"answer":2,"solution":"According to their SRP value.","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2017 (5 Nov SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d2139341b085cd7dd00f"},{"id":51106,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"d and f Block Elements","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"Treatment of alkaline <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>KMnO</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></math> solution with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>KI</mi></math> solution oxidizes iodide to:-","options":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msubsup><mi>IO</mi><mn>4</mn><mo>-</mo></msubsup></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>IO</mi><msubsup><mo>-</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>-</mo></msubsup></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><msup><mi>IO</mi><mo>-</mo></msup><mn>2</mn></msub></math>"],"answer":2,"solution":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>KI</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>KMnO</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mo>→</mo><msub><mi>KIO</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>MnO</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>KOH</mi></math>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2020 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"614da748d0a64cd15812803b"},{"id":51107,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"d and f Block Elements","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"When a mixture of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>NaCl</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>Cr</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>7</mn></msub></math> and conc. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>SO</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></math> is heated in a dry test tube, a red vapor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">X</mi></mfenced></math> is evolved. This vapor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">X</mi></mfenced></math> turns an aqueous solution of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>NaOH</mi></math> yellow due to the formation of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Y</mi><mo>.</mo><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">X</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Y</mi><mo>,</mo></math> respectively, are:","options":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>CrCl</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>Na</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>Cr</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>7</mn></msub></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>CrCl</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>Na</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>CrO</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>CrO</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>Cl</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>Na</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>CrO</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>Cr</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mfenced><msub><mi>SO</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mfenced><mn>3</mn></msub></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>Na</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>Cr</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>7</mn></msub></math>"],"answer":2,"solution":"<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><mi>NaCl</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>Cr</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>6</mn><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>SO</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>→</mo><mn>4</mn><msub><mi>NaHSO</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>KHSO</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>CrO</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>Cl</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">X</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>6</mn><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>CrO</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>Cl</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>NaOH</mi><mo>⟶</mo><msub><mi>Na</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>CrO</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Y</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>NaCl</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">X</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>CrO</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>Cl</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Y</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>Na</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>CrO</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></math></p>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2020 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"614da750c27f949d7b311a01"},{"id":51108,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"d and f Block Elements","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"The oxidising ability of the given anions follows the order","options":["$\\mathrm{TiO}_{4}^{4-} \\lt  \\mathrm{VO}_{4}^{3-} \\lt  \\mathrm{CrO}_{4}^{2-} \\lt  \\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{-}$","$\\mathrm{VO}_{4}^{3-} \\lt  \\mathrm{CrO}_{4}^{2-} \\lt  \\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{-} \\lt  \\mathrm{TiO}_{4}^{4-}$","$\\mathrm{CrO}_{4}^{2-} \\lt  \\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{-} \\lt  \\mathrm{VO}_{4}^{3-} \\lt  \\mathrm{TiO}_{4}^{4-}$","$\\mathrm{VO}_{4}^{3-} \\lt  \\mathrm{TiO}_{4}^{4-} \\lt  \\mathrm{CrO}_{4}^{2-} \\lt  \\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{-}$"],"answer":0,"solution":"It is decided by SRP value","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2017 (19 Nov SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d213b1134076d98009fa"},{"id":51109,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"d and f Block Elements","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"The fusion of chromite ore $\\left(\\mathrm{FeCr}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{4}\\right)$ with $\\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\mathrm{CO}_{3}$ in air gives a yellow solution upon addition of water. Subsequent treatment with $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ produces an orange solution. The yellow and orange colours, respectively, are due to the formation of","options":["$\\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\mathrm{CrO}_{4}$ and $\\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{7}$","$\\mathrm{Cr}(\\mathrm{OH})_{3}$ and $\\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{7}$","$\\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\\left(\\mathrm{CO}_{3}\\right)_{3}$ and $\\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\\left(\\mathrm{SO}_{4}\\right)_{3}$","$\\mathrm{Cr}(\\mathrm{OH})_{3}$ and $\\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\mathrm{CrO}_{4}$"],"answer":0,"solution":"$8 \\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\mathrm{CO}_{3}+4 \\mathrm{FeCr}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{4}+7 \\mathrm{O}_{2}\\longrightarrow \\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\mathrm{CrO}_{4}+2 \\mathrm{Fe}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{3}+8 \\mathrm{O}_{2}$\nyellow colour\n$2 \\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\mathrm{CrO}_{4} \\stackrel{2 \\mathrm{H}^{+}}{\\longrightarrow} \\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{7}+2 \\mathrm{Na}^{+}+\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$\nyellow\norange","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2017 (19 Nov SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d213bc7a6aeda9450660"},{"id":51110,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"d and f Block Elements","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"$\\mathrm{MnO}_{4}{ }^{-}$oxidizes (i) oxalate ion in acidic medium at $333 \\mathrm{~K}$ and and (ii) HCl. For balanced chemical equations, the ratios $\\left[\\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{-}: \\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{4}{ }^{2-}\\right]$ in (i) and $\\left[\\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{-}: \\mathrm{HCl}\\right]$ in (ii), respectively, are-","options":["$1: 5$ and $2: 5$","$2: 5$ and $1: 8$","$2: 5$ and $1: 5$","$5: 2$ and $1: 8$"],"answer":1,"solution":"$16 \\mathrm{H}^{+}+2 \\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{-}+5 \\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{4}^{2-} \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm{Mn}^{+2}+10 \\mathrm{CO}_{2}+8 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}\\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{-}: \\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{4}{ }^{2-}=2: 52 \\mathrm{KMnO}_{4}+16 \\mathrm{HCl} \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm{KCl}+2 \\mathrm{MnCl}_{2}+5 \\mathrm{Cl}_{2}+8 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}\\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{-}: \\mathrm{HCl}=2: 16=1: 8$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2011 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d213bb3c6380065e2b89"},{"id":51111,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"d and f Block Elements","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"Among <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Ce</mi><mfenced><mrow><mn>4</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mn>5</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mn>6</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfenced><mo>,</mo><mo> </mo><mo>Nd</mo><mfenced><mrow><mn>4</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mn>6</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfenced><mo>,</mo><mo> </mo><mi>Eu</mi><mfenced><mrow><mn>4</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mn>7</mn></msup><mn>6</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfenced></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Dy</mi><mfenced><mrow><mn>4</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mn>10</mn></msup><mn>6</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfenced></math>, the elements having highest and lowest <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn></math>rd ionisation energies, respectively are","options":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Nd</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Ce</mi></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Eu</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Ce</mi></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Eu</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Dy</mi></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Dy</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Nd</mi></math>"],"answer":1,"solution":"<p>For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mi>rd</mi></math> ionisation energy, the electron must be removed from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></math> oxidation state of given metals. The electronic configuration of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Ce</mi></math> is</p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mn>5</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mn>6</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math></p>\n\n<p>The electronic configuration of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Ce</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math> is</p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mn>5</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></math></p>\n\n<p>The electronic configuration of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Nd</mi></math> is</p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mn>6</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math></p>\n\n<p>The electronic configuration of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Nd</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math> is</p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></math></p>\n\n<p>The electronic configuration of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Eu</mi></math> is</p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mn>7</mn></msup><mn>6</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math></p>\n\n<p>The electronic configuration of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Eu</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math> is</p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mn>7</mn></msup></math></p>\n\n<p>The electronic configuration of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Dy</mi></math> is</p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mn>10</mn></msup><mn>6</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math></p>\n\n<p>The electronic configuration of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Dy</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mn>10</mn></msup></math> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Eu</mi></math> will have the maximum <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mi>rd</mi></math> ionisation enthalphy, because the electron has to be removed from stable half-filled <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mrow><mn>4</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mn>7</mn></msup></mrow></mfenced><msup><mi>Eu</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math> configuration, whereas <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Ce</mi></math> will have the lowest <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mi>rd</mi></math> ionisation enthalpy as the electron has to be removed from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></math>-orbital.</p>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2018 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"614da696e48b07ebfce8887c"}]}